NAME
       passwd - change user password

SYNOPSIS
       passwd [options] [LOGIN]

DESCRIPTION
       passwd changes passwords for user accounts.  A normal user may only
       change the password for their own account, while the superuser may
       change the password for any account.  passwd also changes password
       aging information.

       The user is first prompted for the current password (the superuser is
       exempt).  The password is hashed and compared against the stored value.
       The user is then prompted twice for the replacement password; both
       entries must match.

       New passwords are hashed with yescrypt using a cryptographically random
       salt drawn from the kernel entropy pool, and the account's last-change
       date is updated.

OPTIONS
       -a, --all
              Only with -S: show status for all users.

       -d, --delete
              Delete the password (make it empty).

       -e, --expire
              Expire the password, forcing a change at the next login.

       -i, --inactive DAYS
              Set the number of days after expiry before the account is
              disabled.

       -k, --keep-tokens
              Change the password only if it has expired.

       -l, --lock
              Lock the account's password by prefixing it with '!'.

       -n, --mindays DAYS
              Set the minimum number of days between password changes.

       -q, --quiet
              Quiet mode.

       -r, --repository REPO
              Change the password in REPO.  Only the "files" repository is
              supported.

       -R, --root DIR
              Apply changes to the account databases under DIR.

       -S, --status
              Report password status: name, status (P usable, L locked, NP no
              password), last change, and the min, max, warn and inactive
              fields (in days).

       -u, --unlock
              Unlock the account's password.

       -w, --warndays DAYS
              Set the number of days of warning before a password expires.

       -x, --maxdays DAYS
              Set the maximum number of days a password stays valid.

       -h, --help
              Display help and exit.

       Options that change aging information or lock, unlock or delete a
       password may be used by the superuser only.

FILES
       /etc/passwd   user account database
       /etc/shadow   encrypted passwords and aging information

SEE ALSO
       login(1), su(1), adduser(8)

